    "updated_at": "2020-01-25T22:36:15Z",
    "body": "Regarding configuration: I suppose sth a bit smarter than simple patterns can be needed, as it's fairly likely that one would like to extract and use some part of … some field. So sth like regexp/pattern with named groups may be needed. But before going deeper into parsing context name it may also make sense to look whether some useful information is available in other fields.\r\n\r\nCould sb quote example of ~/.kube/config entries for some important cloud providers (mangle some values if yuo like, only general format matters). Maybe looking at a couple of them will show some patterns.\r\n\r\nTo start those examples, here is snippet from (private) openshift (or rather OKD) installation I use(I bet public openshift can be similar):\r\n```\r\n- context:\r\n    cluster: devopshft-cloud-myorg-local:8443\r\n    namespace: mk-experiments\r\n    user: marcin/devopshft-cloud-myorg-local:8443\r\n  name: mk-experiments/devopshft-cloud-myorg-local:8443/marcin\r\n```\r\n(here ```marcin``` is my username, ``mk-experiments`` is openshift project aka kubernetes namespace, whole installation has console on devopshft.cloud.myorg.local:8443). From non-trivial parsing point of view this variant of course cries to drop port number extract ``marcin`` (and maybe portless machine) somehow, but in general all information is more easily available from context fields than from context name.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
